# PAM configuration file for Cyrus IMAP service.
#
# This file defines which PAM stacks Cyrus IMAP uses for authentication
# and account management. It relies on the system's PAM configuration
# (typically located in /etc/pam.d/).
#
# IMPORTANT SECURITY NOTES:
# 1. If Cyrus is used in a setup where users do NOT have local OS accounts
#    (e.g., they exist only in a remote directory like LDAP/AD), you MUST
#    ensure a PAM module that permits access (like pam_permit) is included
#    in the account stack, otherwise login will fail due to missing user info.
# 2. SASL (Simple Authentication and Security Layer) accesses PAM modules
#    via the saslauthd daemon.
# 3. If PAM is used for SASL, it can handle plaintext passwords securely
#    if the transport is encrypted, but be aware of local PAM audit trails.

# --- Authentication Stack ---
# The common PAM authentication stack to include for Cyrus IMAP.
# This file is typically /etc/pam.d/common-auth or similar, depending on
# the OS distribution. It handles password verification against local
# shadow files, LDAP, Kerberos, etc.
# Default: common-auth
@include {{ common_auth | default('common-auth') }}

# --- Account Management Stack ---
# The common PAM account management stack to include for Cyrus IMAP.
# This file is typically /etc/pam.d/common-account. It checks if the
# user account is valid, not expired, and allowed to log in at this time.
# Default: common-account
@include {{ common_account | default('common-account') }}